  "summary_text": "Kyle, yesterday was a mixed story across the two stores, with Port Washington running hot and Hicksville struggling\u2014and the divergence tells us something important about capacity discipline.\n\nPort Washington delivered a standout day: $3,615 revenue on five active groomers, yielding $520 per groomer\u2014above your 30-day baseline of $496. That's real productivity. Grooming revenue jumped 68% week-over-week, driven by strong core groom attach (19 grooms in a single day). The one operational miss was retail, down 54%, which suggests front-of-house didn't capitalize on foot traffic\u2014worth a conversation with that team. Month-to-date, Port is down 7% in absolute dollars but only 2.6% against calendar-adjusted expectation, meaning the shortfall is almost entirely the unfavorable DOW mix in July, not performance. Your 12+ week visit bucket is shrinking (15.5% to 13.3%), a leading indicator that retention is tightening. That's good news.\n\nHicksville is the concern. Yesterday's $672 revenue came from just one active groomer\u2014Julia B at $545, well above her trailing average of $411, but you can't build a store on one person. Appointments dropped 44% week-over-week and add-on attach fell to 20% from 44%, signaling both availability and upsell discipline problems. Month-to-date Hicksville is actually +4.2% vs calendar-adjusted expectation, so there's underlying strength, but the visit-cadence drift is moving the wrong way: the 12+ week gap bucket grew from 10.2% to 12.6%, a stealth churn signal. You need more consistent groomer capacity here.\n\nThe pattern: Port has the people to serve demand; Hicksville doesn't. Today: Port 6 groomers with 0 bather hours, Hicksville 2 groomers with 0 bather hours; next 7 days Port 34 groomer-days and 43 bather hours scheduled, Hicksville 14 groomer-days and zero bather hours."
